## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to model constraints with DP, including exact count, non-adjacency, and reconstruction; this is a common interview pattern for medium-difficulty tasks.

## Key Concepts

- 2D DP with adjacency constraint
- Exact-count optimization
- Backtracking to recover indices

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxPromo(revenue, k, cost){
  const n = revenue.length;
  const NEG = Number.NEGATIVE_INFINITY;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++){
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++){
      // skip i
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i][t], dp[i-1][t]);
      // take i
      if (t > 0){
        const takeFrom = i >= 2 ? dp[i-2][t-1] : (t-1 === 0 ? 0 : NEG);
        const val = takeFrom + revenue[i-1] - cost;
        dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i][t], val);
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction
  let i = n, t = k;
  const picks = [];
  while (i > 0 && t >= 0){
    if (dp[i][t] === dp[i-1][t]){ i--; }
    else { picks.unshift(i); i -= 2; t--; }
  }
  return { best: dp[n][k], picks };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize space to O(k)?
- How to handle ties in reconstruction?

## How It Works

Concise technical explanation of the mechanism.

## Key Points

- **DP State**: local[k], global[k] track ending vs non-ending subarrays
- **Transition**: local[k] = max(local[k]+a[i], global[k-1]+a[i]); global[k] = max(global[k], local[k])
- **Edge Handling**: cap K to n; initialize -inf; handles negative values

## Example

```javascript
function maxRevenue(A, K) {
  const n = A.length;
  if (n === 0 || K <= 0) return 0;
  K = Math.min(K, n);
  const global = new Array(K+1).fill(-Infinity);
  const local = new Array(K+1).fill(-Infinity);
  global[0] = 0;
  for (let x of A) {
    for (let k = K; k >= 1; k--) {
      local[k] = Math.max((local[k] !== -Infinity ? local[k] + x : -Infinity), global[k-1] + x);
      global[k] = Math.max(global[k], local[k]);
    }
  }
  return global[K];
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
